As a measure of the general living standard, per capita real GDP has the following limitations.
•	It cannot reflect the problem of uneven income distribution;
•	It cannot reflect the uses of output;
•	It does not include the value of unpaid services produced by households for self-consumption;
•	It does not consider the undesirable effects of production which lower living standards;
•	It does not count the value of leisure.
(Any TWO of the above or other reasonable explanations. 2 marks × 2 = 4 marks)

The following are the effects of inflation:
•	The cost of living rises and the purchasing power of money drops;
•	The standard of living may fall;
•	Resources are reallocated;
•	Volume of exports decreases while volume of imports increases.
(Any TWO of the above with brief explanations. 2 marks each.)

The following are the effects of deflation:
•	The cost of living falls and the purchasing power of money rises;
•	The standard of living may rise;
•	Resources are reallocated;
•	Volume of exports increases while volume of imports decreases.
(Any TWO of the above with brief explanations. 2 marks each.)

The government can: 
introduce favourable tax policies to attract foreign direct investment to the country;
improve human capital in order to improve labour productivity;
uphold the rule of law to improve confidence in investing in the country;
develop the infrastructure, such as transport and communications networks;
support research and development in order to strengthen the competitiveness of domestic products.
(Any THREE of the above with brief explanation, 2 marks each. Accept other reasonable answers.)
